Name: rowTag

Type: property

Syntax: set the rowTag of <line> of <field> to <string>

Summary:
Associates a string tag with a paragraph (line) of a field, without
affecting its visible content.

Associations: field

Introduced: 0.9.14

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop, server

Example:
set the rowTag of line 1 of field "data" to "row-42"

Example:
put the rowTag of line 3 of field "data"

Example:
-- Tag each line with a database primary key
repeat with tLine = 1 to the number of lines of field "results"
   set the rowTag of line tLine of field "results" to line tLine of tKeys
end repeat

Example:
-- Locate a tagged row after sorting or reordering
repeat with tLine = 1 to the number of lines of field "results"
   if the rowTag of line tLine of field "results" is tTargetKey then
      select line tLine of field "results"
      exit repeat
   end if
end repeat

Parameters:
string (string):
Any string. Set to empty to remove the tag from the paragraph.

Value:
The <rowTag> of a line of a field returns a string, or empty if no tag
has been set.

Description:
Use the <rowTag> property to attach an invisible identifier to a
paragraph of a field. Unlike the <metadata> property, which can be
applied to any chunk (character, word, or line), <rowTag> is strictly
a paragraph-level attribute — it is always attached to the whole line,
not to individual characters within it.

A typical use is to associate each visible line of a field with a
primary key or record identifier from a database, so that the correct
record can be retrieved even after the field has been sorted, filtered,
or otherwise reordered.

**Behaviour when lines are inserted or removed**

The <rowTag> travels with the paragraph that holds it:

- Copying a line between fields with `put` preserves the tag on the
  destination paragraph.
- Inserting a blank line *before* a tagged line (via the keyboard or
  `put return before line N of field`) leaves the tag on the original
  content; the new empty line receives no tag.
- Splitting a line in the middle (pressing Return part-way through)
  leaves the tag on the first half; the second half receives no tag.
- Joining two lines (deleting the paragraph break between them) keeps
  the tag of whichever paragraph retains its content.

**Persistence**

The <rowTag> is included in the <styledText> export of a field, under
the key `"rowtag"` in each paragraph's array entry. Assigning
<styledText> back to a field restores the tags. The property is also
saved and loaded as part of the stack file.

References: styledText (property), metadata (property),
flagged (property), textAlign (property), spaceAbove (property),
spaceBelow (property)

Tags: text processing

